\n        edges.append((w, u, v))\\n    edges.sort(key=lambda x: (x[0], x[1], x[2]))\\n    parent = {node: node for node in nodes}\\n    def find(x):\\n        while parent[x] != x:\\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\\n            x = parent[x]\\n        return x\\n    def union(x, y):\\n        rx, ry = find(x), find(y)\\n        if rx == ry:\\n            return False\\n        parent[ry] = rx\\n        return True\\n    mst_edges = []\\n    total_weight = 0.0\\n    for w, u, v in edges:\\n        if union(u, v):\\n            mst_edges.append([u, v, w])\\n            total_weight += w\\n            if len(mst_edges) == len(nodes) - 1:\\n                break\\n    if len(mst_edges) != len(nodes) - 1:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Graph is disconnected\\\")\\n    return {\\\"mst_edges\\\": mst_edges, \\\"total_weight\\\": total_weight}\n\nReplace double quotes: In raise line: `raise ValueError(\\\"Graph is disconnected\\\")` -> `raise ValueError(\\\"Graph is disconnected\\\")` becomes `raise ValueError(\\\"Graph is disconnected\\\")` but we need to escape each \" inside the JSON string. So we write `raise ValueError(\\\"Graph is disconnected\\\")` as `raise ValueError(\\\"Graph is disconnected\\\")`? Wait, careful: In the JSON representation, to have a double quote character in the string, you write \\\". So if we want the Python code to contain `\"Graph is disconnected\"`, we need to write that in the JSON string as `\\\"Graph is disconnected\\\"`. So in the JSON string, the characters are: backslash, double-quote, G, r, a, p, h, space, i, s, space, d, i, s, c, o, n, n, e, c, t, e, d, backslash, double-quote. Similarly for return line: `{\\\"mst_edges\\\": mst_edges, \\\"total_weight\\\": total_weight}`.\n\nBut note: The backslash before the double quote in the JSON string is an escape for the double quote. So we need to write the double quotes that are part of the Python code as \\\". So we'll modify the implementation string accordingly.\n\nLet's construct the full implementation string with escapes.\n\nI'll write it as a literal Python string in my mind, then escape it for JSON.\n\nThe Python code (as a string) should be:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    core_distance = case['core_distance']\n    distances = case['distances']\n    edges = []\n    for d in distances:\n        u, v, dist = d\n        cu = core_distance[u]\n        cv = core_distance[v]\n        w = max(cu, cv, dist)\n        if u > v:\n            u, v = v, u\n        edges.append((w, u, v))\n    edges.sort(key=lambda x: (x[0], x[1], x[2]))\n    parent = {node: node for node in nodes}\n    def find(x):\n        while parent[x] != x:\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\n            x = parent[x]\n        return x\n    def union(x, y):\n        rx, ry = find(x), find(y)\n        if rx == ry:\n            return False\n        parent[ry] = rx\n        return True\n    mst_edges = []\n    total_weight = 0.0\n    for w, u, v in edges:\n        if union(u, v):\n            mst_edges.append([u, v, w])\n            total_weight += w\n            if len(mst_edges) == len(nodes) - 1:\n                break\n    if len(mst_edges) != len(nodes) - 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"Graph is disconnected\")\n    return {\"mst_edges\": mst_edges, \"total_weight\": total_weight}\n\nNow, to embed this as a JSON string, we need to escape newlines as \\n and double quotes as \\\". Also, any backslash that is part of the code (there are none except possibly in string literals but we don't have) needs to be escaped. So we replace newline in the code with \\n, and replace \" with \\\".\n\nLet's produce the JSON string line by line, but collapsed into a single line with \\n. Actually, we can keep it as a multi-line JSON string? No, JSON strings cannot have literal newlines; they must be escaped. So we must have \\n for each line break.\n\nSo the final implementation string will be: \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    core_distance